      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2015 The Obesity Society.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "The aim of this study was to correlate different degrees of excess weight with the expression of podocyte-associated messenger RNAs (mRNAs) in urine."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "The sample comprised 83 patients with overweight or obesity class I, II, or III and 18 healthy controls. The expression of nephrin, podocin, podocalyxin, α-actinin-4, α3β1integrin, vascular endothelial growth factor, and transforming growth factor-beta (TGF-β1 ) mRNA in urine was quantified with the real-time polymerase chain reaction. mRNA expression was correlated with body mass index, the metabolic syndrome, albuminuria, and inflammation."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Adults with obesity class III had higher levels of serum lipids, glucose, HbA1C, insulin resistance, and C-reactive protein (P\u2009<\u20090.05), with 85% of the subjects meeting criteria for the metabolic syndrome (P\u2009<\u20090.001 vs. other groups). Urinary podocyte-associated mRNAs were higher in adults with obesity class III than in other groups (P\u2009<\u20090.05). Patients with overweight or obesity class I or II also had higher levels of podocyte mRNAs than controls: nephrin (P\u2009=\u20090.021), α-actinin-4 (P\u2009=\u20090.014), α3β1integrin (P\u2009=\u20090.036), and TGF-β1 (P\u2009=\u20090.005). Metabolic syndrome, hyperinsulinemia, and C-reactive protein were correlated with podocyturia, but only higher insulin levels were related regardless of obesity."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "Severe obesity and hyperinsulinemia were associated with higher urinary expression of podocyte-associated mRNAs, even at normal urinary albumin excretion rates."
          }
        ]
      },
